jealously guarded their autonomy  that the new monarch's authority  extended over them  on january 14 1559  the tower also marked the starting point  of elizabeth's coronation procession  city streets were decorated with silk  and tapestries and the queen processed  from it in a chariot accompanied by  lords and ladies in crimson velvet and  trumpeters wearing red gowns  as she made her way to london's urban  center she was met with sites to behold  everywhere  public performances were staged in  nearly every london neighborhood  residents paid homage to the new queen  by acting out skits singing songs of  praise and reading simple poems and  verse composed especially for the  occasion  because the tower was such a potent  symbol of power and authority it  periodically came under attack  from those who wished to air grievances  or achieve a particular political goal  for instance  in 1349  the country experienced an outbreak of  the bubonic plague otherwise known as  the black death which killed close to  half of london's population  approximately 70 000 people were said to  have died  periodic outbreaks of the illness  occurred through the 1360s  the economic dislocation wrought by the  worldwide pandemic led to extreme  measures  including limits on wages and new taxes  not surprisingly this produced  widespread discontent  in  1377 king richard ii came to the throne  at the age of 10.  his policies were guided by his uncle  john of gaunt who administered a  despised poll tax on all english people  over the age of 15.  widespread resistance to paying led to  the creation of royal commissions to  investigate how extensive tax evasion  was  and to extract payments from the  holdouts  in may 1381  a commission set out for the county of  essex just northeast of london  it was met by a series of uprisings led  by the priest john ball and a humble  artisan named watt tyler that came to be  known as the peasants revolt  ball had acquired a reputation as a  proponent of radical ideas questioning  the very notion of a feudal hierarchy  he also wondered aloud why if all  descended from adam and eve there should  be distinctions of rank  emboldened by such critiques and fueled  by economic and social discontent the  frustrated peasantry considered its  options  in mid-june 1381  some sixty thousand armed peasants  converged upon black heath to the south  and the east of the tower of london  their request to meet with the king as  part of their demands for greater social  equality were refused  so  they marched directly into london proper  along the way  they destroyed the homes of the great  and the wealthy and they released the  inmates of marshall c prison in  southwark  the symbolic gestures of attacking signs  of privilege continued for several days  among the grandest targets was john of  gaunt's london residence the savoy  there the peasants killed john's  servants tore gold and silver cloth to  pieces crushed his jewels destroyed his  tapestries and threw countless numbers  of his possessions into the river  they also lit an enormous bonfire  fueled by gunpowder reducing the palace  to ruins  turning back to the king  the crowd returned to the tower and  camped outside its gates richard ii  recognizing that he needed to do  something agreed to meet the peasants in  mile end to the east of the city  while he was there supposedly granting  concessions  another group broke into the tower  destroying kitchens bed chambers halls  and armories they even ransacked the  king's mother's apartments destroying  furniture and tearing her bed clothes to  shreds  most significantly the unhappy peasants  entered the white tower where four  members of the king's government  including the archbishop of canterbury  and the king's treasurer were dragged  out of the chapel and beheaded  their heads were placed on pikes and  paraded through the city  the revolt largely ended the next day  watt tyler was stabbed to death by the  mayor of london  and the king's supporters were able to  defuse the crowd  the revolt's leaders were ultimately  executed and many of the king's promises  of economic and social reform when  unrealized  but the burden of the poll tax was  eliminated for ordinary englishmen and  women  let's now look at a totally different  kind of challenge to the tower's  symbolic stature this time in the 17th  century  in the decades before the english civil  war from 1642 to 1651  the election of the lord mayor for the  city of london was frequently  accompanied by a street performance  known as the lord mayor's show  the show's mixed theatrical performances  with dance music speeches and firework  displays  and they were scripted by playwrights  one of these playwrights was thomas  haywood  who wrote seven of the shows between  1631  and 1639  many of the presentations offered some  praise of the monarch  but they also sought to highlight the  power of the lord mayor and the  political autonomy of the city of london  in some cases  haywood's shows  challenged what has been referred to as  the royal ideology of the tower of  london  this signaled growing tension between  the king at the time charles the first  and others who thought that royal  authority might be getting just a bit  out of hand  in the 1635 show london's harbor of  health and happiness  the tower is depicted  not as a symbol of royal splendor but  rather as a site of royal tyranny where  people have been imprisoned unfairly  the most notable example of this was  richard the duke of gloucester who in  1483  had become the protector of his brother  edward iv 12 year old royal son edward v  hungry for power richard imprisoned the  young king and his brother in the tower  and allegedly had them murdered before  being proclaimed king himself  in 1638 the show the port or harbor of  piety referred to the tower as a space  where the monarch imprisoned religious  dissenters  most notably during the 16th century  when the battle between protestants and  catholics raged  haywood's representations of the virgin  mary revealed the author's support for  catholics and served as a reminder of  the tower's history in persecuting and  torturing roman catholics during the  reigns of edward vi  from 1547  until 1553 and elizabeth the first from  1558 until 1603  the tower faced other affronts during  the 19th century  including age fire  and stench  by the time queen victoria came to the  throne in 1837  years of wear and tear on the tower  meant that it like any ancient structure  was in need of repair  the first duke of wellington arthur  wellesley was the man who oversaw many  of these needed improvements  wellington was best known as the  military leader who defeated napoleon  bonaparte at the battle of waterloo  in recognition of his service  he was made constable of the tower of  london a sinicure  that came with a sizeable annual income  most beneficiaries of this office  treated it as a preferment  wellington took it seriously rooting out  corruption and ensuring that the tower  was an appropriate symbol of the nation  among the reforms he implemented at the  tower  was an attempt to professionalize the  royal household guards the yeoman waters  or beefeaters  created in the middle ages they were by  the 19th century essentially  unscrupulous tour guides extorting money  from visitors and using the autonomy of  the tower precinct to avoid paying their  debts  wellington changed this  by appointing ex-military men of good  character to represent the queen and the  nation effectively  changes to the tower were also prompted  by two specific developments  a massive fire in the middle of the 19th  century and the recognition that the  stagnant water in the tower's moat was  becoming a major nuisance and a major  health hazard  let's turn first to the fire  on the evening of october 30th 1841  a chimney fire in the complex quickly  spread  the towers fire engines and water supply  were inadequate to combat the threat  as the evening wore on the flames  engulfed the armory storehouse and  threatened the martin tower where the  crown jewels were stored the most  valuables were in fact safe  newspapers described the blaze as a  truly national calamity that exceeded  and grandeur even the great fire of the  house of commons which had occurred in  1834  plans guided by the government and the  monarch were laid to rebuild some  structures to rationalize others and  ultimately to plan for the future  foremost among wellington's initiatives  was a plan to replace the complex's 17th  century brick and stone storehouse now  completely destroyed with a new more  commodious barracks for the soldiers who  resided in the tower complex  known as the waterloo barracks in honor  of his military victory against napoleon  bonaparte the living quarters were  completed in 1845.  wellington also closed the pubs that had  previously been open in the tower  replacing them with proper facilities  for feeding soldiers and thereby  allowing officers to socialize in a  class segregated mess  around the same time wellington  undertook to drain the tower moat  rather than being cleansed by the river  thames the moat retained the human waste  the tower's residents emptied into it on  a daily basis  it was a breeding ground for waterborne  disease  and unbefitting a historic landmark  thanks to wellington's efforts the moat  was delivered from putrid animal and  excrementitious  matter  in the words of one contemporary and  replaced with dry land that extended the  tower's grounds creating space for  military drills and other activities  but the tower was a target of  desecration as well as beautification  those who supported either irish  independence in the 19th century or the  unification of the northern and southern  counties of ireland in the 20th struck  on several different occasions  during the 19th century many in ireland  began to question british domination and  called for home rule  among those who embraced a more radical  approach the quest for  self-determination justified acts of  violence  in 1885 activists seeking to establish  an independent irish republic were  concluding a four-year campaign of  bombing that included the tower of  london as a predominantly symbolic  target  part of the dynamite war a bomb was  detonated in the white tower on january  24 1885  at around the same time as bombs were  also exploding in parliament  at the tower complex windows and walls  were blown out and a large-scale fire  that the tower fire brigade contained  erupted  but this was not of course the final  time the tower came under attack by  irish nationalists  in 1922  britain granted independence to the  southern portion of ireland while six  northern counties with a majority  protestant population remained a part of  the united kingdom  this accommodation stoked outrage among  more militant activists in the irish  independence movement  in 1969  after several decades of relative quiet  the paramilitary organization the irish  republican army responded to perceived  injustices and the protestant  persecution of catholics by inaugurating  a further campaign of violence  this campaign was most associated with  two pub bombings in the english cities  of birmingham and guilford in 1974.  hundreds were injured and more than two  dozen were killed  there was also another attack on the  tower of london that same year this one  widely attributed to the ira but not  claimed by it  by this time the tower was no longer a  royal residence nor was it a center of  government  but it remained a symbol of british  authority as well as an important  tourist destination and so  it remained a potentially value-rich  target for terrorist acts  at around two in the afternoon on july  17  1974 at least one bomb exploded in the  white tower it was thought to have been  placed under the royal george cannon in  one of the public exhibition spaces  the cannon was blown off its base  multiple suits of metal armor were  destroyed and wood and other debris flew  through the air  41 people were injured and one 47 year  old woman was killed  a librarian named dorothy household  some of the injuries were actually quite  severe a six-year-old boy from new  zealand lost his leg in the blast  debate in the house of commons on the  night of the explosion revealed just how  raw emotions were  some members rose to offer condolences  and expressed concern that londoners and  tourists alike were vulnerable to  terrorist acts  others such as mp william malloy stated  defiantly that attacks on symbolic  structures such as the tower would not  bend the will of the people  precisely because the tower is so rich  in history and symbolic in meaning it  can elicit very strong reactions as i  walked the grounds and climbed one of  the many flights of stairs during a  recent visit i couldn't help but think  about the many who have lived and died  sometimes quite notoriously  within the towers carefully manicured  precincts  contained within the complex are stories  of the rich and the poor the fortunate  and the unfortunate and the damned and  the saved
 

